Found during k113 (worker). When an Http upstream is stopped (session ended, session_id gone) while a refresh is between two pages and no start follows, the next page goes out with no Mcp-Session-Id. Harmless -- the remote refuses it, the refresh fails at debug level and the manifest is kept -- but the request should not be sent at all. Done: a request that is not the start's own and finds the upstream stopped fails at once as stopped, without a POST. Test in t/http.t.

Fixed: Http::_http_request_p -- a request that is not the start's own and finds the upstream stopped with no start under way fails at once as "upstream NAME: stopped", no POST. Test in t/http.t, red 2/14 on HEAD (page 2 was POSTed without a session); green under EV and Poll. Worker's choice: the check is state eq 'stopped', so it covers calls too: a call a stop overtakes between its start and its request now fails as stopped instead of going out without a session (a stateless remote could have served it) -- open a card only if that is unwanted. -> review.

